<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Send obj/ and bin/ somewhere other than the source tree.

      This has to live in Directory.Build.props, not in the environment: MSBuild
      reads ArtifactsPath as an ordinary property either way, but restore works
      out where to put project.assets.json during early evaluation, before an
      environment-supplied value has taken effect. Set it only in compose and
      the build output lands in /artifacts while restore still litters
      MedSign.Api/obj and MedSign.Tests/obj.

      Keeping those directories out of the tree is not tidiness. The tree is
      bind-mounted from the host and watched by polling, and dotnet watch's
      poller dies outright if files appear underneath it mid-enumeration. Build
      output is by far the largest source of such files. See run-backend.sh.

      MEDSIGN_ARTIFACTS is what the container passes in, but it cannot be the
      only rule: not every evaluation of these projects inherits it. dotnet
      watch scans the project graph in its own MSBuild invocation, and that one
      arrives without it, which was enough to keep dropping generated files back
      into the watched tree. Falling back to the mounted volume when it is
      simply there is the part that cannot be lost.

      Outside the container neither applies, and there is no watcher to protect,
      so a local artifacts folder is fine.
    -->
    <ArtifactsPath Condition="'$(MEDSIGN_ARTIFACTS)' != ''">$(MEDSIGN_ARTIFACTS)</ArtifactsPath>
    <ArtifactsPath Condition="'$(ArtifactsPath)' == '' AND Exists('/artifacts')">/artifacts</ArtifactsPath>

    <!--
      Last resort, and the reason it is not simply a folder next to this file:
      on Linux we are in the workshop container, where a folder next to this
      file is inside the bind mount the watcher is polling. Somewhere in dotnet
      watch's own project evaluation both rules above still come up empty, and a
      handful of generated files kept reappearing in the tree because of it.
      Sending the fallback to /tmp ends the whole class of problem rather than
      chasing the last evaluation that escapes.

      On Windows there is no watcher and no /tmp worth using, so a local folder
      is the right answer there.
    -->
    <ArtifactsPath Condition="'$(ArtifactsPath)' == '' AND '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT'">/tmp/medsign-artifacts</ArtifactsPath>
    <ArtifactsPath Condition="'$(ArtifactsPath)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts</ArtifactsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
